    "body": "Salvador, Brazil. ( Stock image by Anna ART. )\nBy: Staff Writer November 16, 2026\nScotia Metals (CSE: SMET) has signed an option to acquire the Nordestina lithium exploration project in northeastern Brazil for $2 million (C$2.85 million), expanding its search for the battery metal beyond Nova Scotia.\nThe Vancouver-based junior can acquire an indirect 100% interest in the 16,566-hectare property in Bahia state by May 31, 2028. It must pay $10,000 monthly during the option period, with those payments separate from the purchase price, but has no minimum exploration spending commitment and can withdraw on 30 days’ notice.\n“Nordestina is the kind of ground we look for, with lithium minerals at surface,” CEO Rodrigo Roso said in a release Monday. “The Nordestina option complements our ongoing work at Acadia and gives us a second prospective hard-rock lithium project to evaluate.”\nThe agreement gives Scotia a second lithium exploration project alongside Acadia in Nova Scotia. However, Nordestina remains untested by drilling, with no mineral resource established and laboratory confirmation pending for the minerals identified visually during fieldwork.\nExploration\nScotia must also cover documented exploration costs of up to $210,000 incurred by R-Tek International DMCC. The exercise price is subject to adjustments for net debt or contingencies.\nThe transaction requires the nine mineral permits to be transferred into a Brazilian project company, including approval from Brazil’s National Mining Agency and applicable Canadian Securities Exchange acceptance.\nMapping identified suspected spodumene at 100 locations across an area about 3.3 km long and 1.4 km wide within one permit. The observations included 64 outcrops and 36 occurrences in loose rock and do not establish a continuous mineralized body.\nInitial results from 1,208 soil samples and 147 rock grab samples are expected in late November, subject to laboratory capacity. Scotia plans to combine the assays with mapping and geophysical data to select initial drill targets.\nScotia shares were 34% stronger at C29.5¢ late Monday, valuing the company at about C$13.4 million. Print\nCOMMODITY: lithium\nREGION: South America\nCOMPANY: Scotia Metals\nRepublish this article",
